# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the mdb-tools ODBC driver, applied to the ODBC provider's
# Access suite (run_fdo_tests.sh --with-odbc-access).
#
# mdb-tools' SQL engine (libmdbsql) allocates a glib object for every WHERE expression it
# evaluates and never frees it, so each filtered query costs one 304-byte chunk:
#
#     Direct leak of 304 byte(s) in 1 object(s) allocated from:
#         #1 g_malloc0
#         #2 mdb_sql_eval_expr       (libmdbsql.so.3)
#         #3 mdb_sql_run_query
#         #4 SQLExecute              (libmdbodbc.so)
#
# The same picture was measured for mdb_sql_add_and, which the AND-ed filters reach. The
# allocation is never an FDO object: a standalone C client that opens the same .mdb through
# the driver, frees every statement, disconnects and frees every handle leaks the same way,
# and the chunks survive SQLDisconnect.
#
# Matching is by module rather than by function because these chunks are allocated by glib,
# which has no unwind tables, so how much of the mdbtools stack LSan can name varies with
# library load order. A leak in FDO does not carry a libmdbsql frame and is still reported.
#
# The driver is a test dependency only; the records are documented in
# docs/odbc-linux-support.md and docs/fdo-provider-memory-leaks.md.
leak:libmdbsql.so
